@page "/settings"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz

@inject QuizSettings QuizSettings
@inject QuizController Controller
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>BgQuiz — Settings</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-3" style="max-width:800px">
    <h1>Settings</h1>
    @* The lead promises persistence, not visible effect: one setting below
       deliberately shows itself on the next page rather than on this one (the
       fold — see its own fine print), so a blanket "takes effect immediately"
       would be a claim the page then breaks. "Kept as you make it" is the part
       that is true of all three, and it is also the part a reader is looking
       for when they wonder where the Save button is. *@
    <p class="lead">
        Every change here is kept as you make it and remembered in this
        browser — there is nothing to save.
    </p>

    @* Gated on hydration so no control paints a default the stored settings are
       about to overwrite. On any ordinary visit the task is already complete and
       this branch is taken on the first render anyway. *@
    @if (_hydrated)
    {
        <fieldset class="mb-4">
            <legend class="h5">The board</legend>

            <fieldset class="mb-3">
                <legend class="form-label mb-1">Your home board is on the</legend>
                <div class="form-check">
                    <input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="homeBoardSide"
                           id="settingsSideRight"
                           checked="@QuizSettings.HomeBoardOnRight"
                           @onchange="@(() => SetHomeBoardOnRightAsync(true))" />
                    <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsSideRight">Right</label>
                </div>
                <div class="form-check">
                    <input class="form-check-input" type="radio" name="homeBoardSide"
                           id="settingsSideLeft"
                           checked="@(!QuizSettings.HomeBoardOnRight)"
                           @onchange="@(() => SetHomeBoardOnRightAsync(false))" />
                    <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsSideLeft">Left</label>
                </div>
            </fieldset>

            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsRandomizeSide"
                       checked="@QuizSettings.RandomizeSidePerProblem"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetRandomizeSidePerProblemAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsRandomizeSide">
                    Use a random side for each problem
                </label>
            </div>
            <div class="form-text">
                In real play the side is not yours to choose, and a position you
                have seen before is harder to recognise mirrored. Each problem
                draws its own side, and keeps it while you answer and read the
                solution. The choice above applies whenever this is off.
            </div>

            @* The maximize mode (issue #41), and the ONLY control for it — ruled
               (SPEC-quiz-view.md §3, fork D): no on-page toggle on the quiz
               itself, which would be a second write surface for one fact and
               would force QuizSettings to grow the notify plumbing its contract
               defers until a real second consumer exists.

               The fine print leads with what disappears and closes with the
               consequence the model ratified rather than leaving it to be
               discovered: the board is deliberately a different size while
               answering than while reading, so a user who notices it moving is
               reading the feature working, not a bug. Same posture as the fold
               row below, which states its deferral for the same reason. *@
            <div class="form-check mt-3">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsMaximizeBoard"
                       checked="@QuizSettings.MaximizeBoardWhileAnswering"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetMaximizeBoardWhileAnsweringAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsMaximizeBoard">
                    Make the board as large as possible while you answer
                </label>
            </div>
            <div class="form-text">
                While you answer, the score line and the prompt below the board are
                hidden, and the board is drawn without the space the solution's
                analysis panel needs — so it fills the screen. Everything comes
                back when you submit and read the solution, so the board changes
                size between answering and reading.
            </div>
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend class="h5">The navigation panel</legend>
            <div class="form-check">
                <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="settingsKeepNavFolded"
                       checked="@QuizSettings.KeepNavigationPanelFolded"
                       @onchange="@(e => SetKeepNavigationPanelFoldedAsync((bool)e.Value!))" />
                <label class="form-check-label" for="settingsKeepNavFolded">
                    Keep the navigation panel folded
                </label>
            </div>
            @* The deferral is stated, not left to be discovered. Turning this on
               and seeing nothing happen would otherwise read as the setting
               failing — a reading the checkbox alone cannot rule out. The words
               carry the reason too ("how a page starts out"), because that is
               what makes the delay obviously deliberate rather than slow. *@
            <div class="form-text">
                The blue panel down the left of a wide screen starts folded away on
                every page you open, giving its width back to the board. You can
                still open it any time with the strip at the left edge; this only
                decides how a page starts out, so it takes hold as you move on
                from here rather than folding the panel you are using now.
            </div>
        </fieldset>
    }

    @* The way back to a running quiz. Copied from Help — same predicate, same
       markup, same words — rather than designed: the two pages are in the same
       position (reachable from any state, so never redirecting the way Stats
       does) and a user who has met one affordance should meet the same one
       here. Outside the hydration gate on purpose: returning to the quiz has
       nothing to do with which settings have loaded.

       It lives on the page and not in the nav panel because the panel renders
       statically and cannot know a quiz is live — the same constraint that put
       the fold applier in JS. *@
    @if (Controller.HasStarted && !Controller.IsFinished)
    {
        <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="BackToQuiz">
            Back to quiz
        </button>
    }
</div>
